    abstract = {In this work we address the problem of predicting proteinprotein interactions. Its solution can give greater insight in the study of complex diseases, like cancer, and provides valuable information in the study of active small molecules for new drugs, limiting the number of molecules to be tested in laboratory. We model the problem as a binary classiﬁcation task, using a suitable coding of the amino acid sequences. We apply k-Nearest Neighbors classiﬁcation algorithm to the classes of interacting and noninteracting proteins. Results show that it is possible to achieve high prediction accuracy in cross validation. A case study is analyzed to show it is possible to reconstruct a real network of thousands interacting proteins with high accuracy on standard hardware.},
